 approximation is appropriate for a given binomial random variable.","position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"key":"r2VxREetmM"}],"key":"jhWgaUB3H4"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Concept Check","position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"key":"FIkRJsdpbm"}],"identifier":"concept-check","label":"Concept Check","html_id":"concept-check","implicit":true,"key":"LQdLUNZYee"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-e9689e083a3d48b3e46875a14efdf8d0.mjs","model":{"question":"Is the normal approximation appropriate for a binomial random variable with n = 100 and p = 0.05?","choices":[{"correct":false,"text":"Yes—both conditions hold","feedback":"np = 100(0.05) = 5, which is less than 10—the rule of thumb fails."},{"correct":true,"text":"No—np = 5 is less than 10","feedback":"Correct! np = 5 < 10, so the approximation is not appropriate; the distribution is still too skewed right."},{"correct":false,"text":"No—n(1 − p) is less than 10","feedback":"n(1 − p) = 95 is fine, but BOTH conditions must hold, and np = 5 fails."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Check np and n(1 − p) against 10.","explanation":""},"key":"jWL4N1ke5n"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-e9689e083a3d48b3e46875a14efdf8d0.mjs","model":{"question":"Is the normal approximation appropriate for a binomial random variable with n = 400 and p = 0.1?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Yes—np = 40 and n(1 − p) = 360 both exceed 10","feedback":"Correct! np = 40 ≥ 10 and n(1 − p) = 360 ≥ 10, so the approximation is appropriate."},{"correct":false,"text":"No—p = 0.1 is too small for any n","feedback":"Check the arithmetic: np = 400(0.1) = 40, which satisfies the condition."}],"multi":false,"hint":"np = 40 and n(1 − p) = 360.","explanation":""},"key":"z1KI3OFVMP"}],"key":"wRfQ3G7CXD"}],"key":"ngvLDaIxEZ"},"references":{"cite":